Maybe you’ve chopped down some trees, or perhaps scavenged some great pieces, but now you’re wondering how to store this wood properly.
I am going share what you need to know about storing your smoking woods the right way to make sure they’re not wet, moldy, and full of pests.
2 How to store wood you purchased
3 How to store large wood logs outdoors
The storage recommendation is different for both types of wood.
The first rule of storing your wood for smoking is:
The ideal container for your woods is a cardboard box with holes – this way the air can circulate, and your wood won’t go moldy.
But remember, any plastic container you use must have air holes that allow for good air circulation.
Whether it’s large logs or smaller chunks you already cut and split up, you need to stack and store the wood properly and let it season.
The seasoning process, which lasts anywhere between 6 months to a couple of years, is essential in the preparation process of woods for smoking.
If the pieces are about the same size, the wood will age evenly.
In autumn, always clean any leaves that fall on your wood, to avoid any rotting and extra moisture.
Use a moisture meter to check your wood regularly. The moisture level should be under 10% at all times.
The most crucial tip for storing your wood is to make sure it is off the ground. Never place your wood directly on the ground.
Before you start stacking your wood, create a base made of something solid, like wood pallets, concrete slabs, or stone.
You begin by taking some medium-sized logs, cut them in half, and place them with the flat side down to use them as a base.
This “chimney” shaped stacks will be the ends of your woodpile.
Your stacks should be about 4 inches high. This ensures that your smoke woods are stable, as you don’t want to risk them falling over and injuring people or animals.
But, keeping them under cover may make it more aesthetically pleasing to look at. If you want to cover your woods, use a tarp.
The frames are off the ground, so your wood is at a safe distance from dirt and won’t rot.
It doesn’t have to be this way though!
So to store wood chips for smoking and keep them fresh in your garage, you need to:
Step 2: Fill each container with just one type of chips (you won’t be able to tell them apart if you mix them!)
What to watch out for when storing wood for smoking
When you store your wood outdoors, there is always a chance that snakes will make their home between the wood pieces, so watch out when you go to grab some pieces.
For this reason, don’t stack and store wood right up against your home, or you risk a termite infestation in your house.
Whether you decide to store the wood outdoors or you place it in your garage or shed, always make sure to allow for air circulation.
So, next time you come home with your woods, remember the three essential tips: keep wood away from direct sunlight, store it in a dry place, and keep it off the ground!
No, smoked cheese isn’t just the type of food to try during the Holidays; it’s actually a year-round smoked delicacy that you don’t want to miss out on! Different types of wood complement specific kinds of cheese.
Smoked cheese is an excellent alternative to the unsmoked variety because it still has the same flavor profile, but the wood smoke also enhances it.
One thing to keep in mind though is that after you smoke cheese, you can’t eat it right away. It has to rest in the fridge for about two weeks.
3 Best wood for smoking cheese
3.10 Other fruitwoods you can use for smoking cheese
5 How long do you need to smoke cheese?
Indeed, the wood matters when it comes to smoking cheese. The wood that you use for smoking ends up affecting the flavor of the cheese.
If you want to keep it simple, try to use locally sourced fruitwoods and nut woods. These are relatively easy to find and decently priced.
Which cheese can you smoke?
Wood for smoking should be seasoned and cut into chunks or chips so it can fit in your smoker.
Apple is the top fruitwood pick when it comes to smoking cheese. For the same reasons applewood works great for smoking poultry, it works well for all cheese varieties.
If you smoke hard cheeses, it imparts a very light flavor that’s is a subtle hint of fruitiness which is definitely not overpowering.
Lilac smoke wood is pretty hard to get your hands on, but it is a premium wood and most commonly used to smoke cheese.
But, you should note that lilac smoke is extremely light, so you’ll need to use semi-soft cheese.
Hickory is a strong flavored smoking wood, and it’s best for smoking strong cheeses like cheddar. Hickory smoke infuses the cheese with a bacon-like flavor.
The pecan smoke infuses your cheese with a nutty flavor, too, but it’s not an overpowering aroma.
This hardwood is similar to hickory in many ways. Oak is frequently used to smoke bigger and flavorful cuts of meat like beef and lamb.
If you want to add a hint of sweetness to any cheese, sugar maple is an excellent option.
Also, avoid eucalyptus, elm, sycamore, and liquid amber as these are not suitable for smoking due to their bad taste.
It gives the cheese a very earthy flavor, so it’s not best for semi-soft cheeses or any cheese with delicate flavors.
Smoking cheese takes about 2-4 hours, depending on the cheese type. It’s important to maintain light smoke but it must keep coming constantly.
The more cheese, the longer you need to smoke. You can extend the time to 5 hours.
I’m using applewood for this recipe because it is a mild smoking wood, and the cheddar is also a mild cheese that doesn’t need intense smoke flavors. When smoking cheese, you’re using a technique called “cold smoking.” You can’t smoke cheese at high temperatures because it melts, so cold smoking is the way to infuse it with smoke wood flavor slowly.
After 30 minutes, turn over each block of cheese.
Once ready, wrap each cheese block in parchment paper but don’t wrap too tightly.
After a day, take the parchment paper off the cheese and place each block into a sealed ziplock bag. Wait for 10-14 days before consuming the cheese.
Therefore, I recommend you stick to mild flavored woods for semi-soft and firm cheeses and hardwood for hard and semi-hard cheeses.
Feel like getting even more cheesy goodness out of your smoker?
Learn more If you’re here, you’re probably wondering: how cold is too cold to use a smoker, and what is the danger zone?
Many people avoid smoking altogether in the winter. But, you can still smoke in cold weather if you follow some basic principles and stay extra vigilant.
The lowest you can smoke though is -15 C or 5 F, but it is not recommended. In cold weather, the smoker has a hard time reaching an internal temperature of 225 F and the meat can end up in the danger zone (between 40 to 140 F) which causes harmful bacteria to multiply which can cause food poisoning.
1.1 What happens to the smoker when it’s cold?
2.3 A thermometer is a must-have accessory
5 Tips for counteracting cold weather, wind, and precipitation
Can I smoke when it’s cold outside?
There are definitely more challenges, but it’s possible.
Smoking meat is harder when the temperatures are low and the main risk is thawing and smoking in the danger zone. I’ll talk about what the danger zone is and how to avoid it in the next section, don’t worry!
What happens to the smoker when it’s cold?
When it’s warm or hot outside, this happens rather quickly. But, if it’s cold out, the internal temperature may not reach 225F and can even be lower by about 35F.
This means that you might need to use more fuel than usual to be able to maintain proper and safe cooking temperatures. Food safety is a huge issue after all and meat is prone to bacterial growth.
Most pitmasters know about the 40 / 140 rule and the danger zone, but beginners should be extra careful.
Also, the 4-hour rule states that the temperature must rise from 40 f to 140 within 4 hours.
The food is not supposed to thaw for a long time though. So, in case the food (especially meat) thaws for a longer than usual time (4 hours or less), between 40-140 degrees F, it is in the “danger zone.”
If it reaches the temperature of 14oF within 4 hours, then the foodborne bacteria don’t have a chance to multiply like crazy.
So, always keep the 40 / 140 rule in mind when smoking in cold weather. If you avoid those low temps, you can ensure the food is safe to eat.
There are serious effects if you smoke in the danger zone.
That’s why you must also be careful with food handling. You don’t want to cause yourself or others serious gastrointestinal illness.
A thermometer is a must-have accessory
Not sure which thermometer to buy?
Smoking meat is usually much easier and more convenient in the warmer months because if you’re going for a long smoke, you can usually leave the smoker unattended for quite some time. However, that’s not the case with smoking in the cold.
You need to wear lots of warm clothing with many layers to ensure you don’t get frostbite. But also, there is a chance the meat won’t smoke properly, and thus, I recommend only pros try smoking at such low temperatures.
Avoid using thin-walled smokers! Some smokers like the Weber Smokey Mountain or the Kettle have notoriously thin walls and chances are you’ll be very late serving dinner because smoking will take longer and you’ll need more fuel.
Luckily though, once you reach that temp, it’s a bit easier to maintain.
As you already know, it’s best to smoke outdoors when it’s warm and there’s not too much wind.
Place the smoker in a sheltered area of the patio or yard. Don’t move it to an enclosed area because that’s dangerous, but you can add it to a corner where there’s less wind and the rain doesn’t pour down on the smoker.
Always be careful and check the temperature inside the smoker. When it’s cold, the temperature can drop very quickly.
Keep extra fuel on hand, whether that’s charcoal, wood pellets, or more propane. Also, have extra flavored wood smoke in case you run out and want to make sure the meat has a tasty aroma.
When smoking meat at lower temperatures, there is always a risk of your food staying in the danger zone (40 – 140F) for more than the maximum of 4 hours. Thus, there’s a potential safety risk because bacteria can multiply on the meat’s surface.
I recommend reading about food safety information from the official FDA website which offers helpful tips on how to prevent food-borne illness when barbecuing and smoking meat. USDA and other official websites can also give you more info.
